A||N||25||TwisT||18"-plus wheeled Fieros;  where are they?||||5||
Z||000000||TwisT||01-02-2000||01:02 AM||||Hi guys,  just joined the list...  Living in South Dakota,  Fiero's tend to be oddities;  and owning one can be a unique experience.  I am working towards investing in some new rolling stock for my '88 GT.  I have my preferences,  with a minimum of 18" of diameter...<P>Question:  who out there in Fiero-land has pictures of their Fiero,  or other's Fiero,  with 18" wheels?  I have yet to see one...  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||01-02-2000||01:47 AM||||Whew!  18" rims.  16"s are almost common and I've seen some 17"s, but I don't know about 18"s.  Remember that you need to keep the circumference pretty close to stock and I'm not sure you could do that with 18"s.  Also, it would seem like you'd have to have fairly wide tires.  Unless you go to a coil-over suspension, I'm not sure you would have enough room to do it without having half of the tire sticking out into space.  And I hope you've got deep pockets 'cause all of the 18" rims/tires I've seen have been pretty pricy.  I've got quite a collection of pics (Fiero and others) and I only have 1 pic of a Fiero w/18"s and you can't really see the wheels.  It's a buddys car and he tricked it out for show only.  What are your intentions?||||reg||1
Z||000002||TwisT||01-02-2000||02:32 AM||||...You are right,  the 18" wheels can be VERY spendy;  but many applications now can be bought for under $1500 (if you look around).  Size wise,  they will fit...  it's just a matter of plus-sizing the tires.  For that matter,  I've discussed sizing and price with Discount Tire Direct...  and even could use 19" wheels for the rear (Konig Tantrum's in this case) and 18"'s for the front.  Total cost for this?  About $2200.  Not cheap,  but thousands cheaper than other packages.  VERY trick,  and not for the orthidonticly challenged!<P>My situation is somewhat unique;  using Ryane's coil-over's in the rear and 2" drop spindles in the front on my '88 GT.  Extra backspacing in the rear will promote a wider rim,  as will the added diameter.  It would be really nice to see a 18" wheel-equiped Fiero before I plunk down the hard-earned dough (just as a selling point;  would buy the dang things anyway!).  Maybe someone out there's got one...  =)  ||||reg||14
Z||000003||Shiner||01-02-2000||03:13 AM||||I have heard running 18 inch rims will drag the bearings.  17's are safe to use, they won't screw up the bearings.||||reg||4
Z||000004||TwisT||01-02-2000||03:41 AM||||...See,  I've heard this too from someone.  I cannot see the logic in it.  Saying that an 18" wheel will ruin bearings and a 17" won't doesn't make sense to me.  Bearing wear would not be caused by or related to rim diameter,  nor tire side-wall height (although a definate contributor to a "harsh" ride).  What IS related is suspension modifications (or the lack there-of) affecting how much stress is transmitted from the road to the bearing through the wheel and tire.  Too much stiffness in the suspension can cause excessive stress on the bearings,  causing them to go out...<P>Remedy?  Well,  the easy one is to modify the suspension (spring rate,  dampening,  etc.) to allow minimal travel,  yet dampening adjusted to "stiff but livable" settings.  This is easily done with coil-overs...  <P>The other way is to upgrade the bearings to ones with a higher load rating.  Of course,  doing this AND installing coil-overs is probably the best way...  when I get my coil-overs on with the new rims/ tires,  I'll let you guys know if I have any sort of problems.    ||||reg||2
Z||000005||cobra427||01-02-2000||04:22 AM||||Heres a thought that you might consider.. The larger the rim, the smaller the sidewall on the tire. which means that with a 18" rim, you'll probably have a 35series tire if you are going to stay close to the original size tire.That doesn't give you much leway when you hit that pot hole or rut in the road doing 50-70mph. They have been known to be damaged. Putting out 5 big ones to replace a rim is not fun at least for most of us anyway.   just a thought||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||01-02-2000||08:38 AM||||Here's a thought after reading your reply.  If you use spacers and wider rims (I can't really see 7" wide rims if you're gonna keep the total circumference the same), you're surely gonna have half of your tire/rim outside the wheel well.  Maybe that's the look you're going for, but if not...Just a thought.  I'm not trying to dicourage you.  If you think it looks good and have the cash to make it work, go for it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||01-02-2000||08:40 AM||||If this is not going to be a daily driver, and is mainly for show or performance driving, and you have the money to burn, than go for it. I'm sure there's some tire combination that will work. For a regular street driven Fiero, I'd get something less exotic, like the 17 inchers. Dang, you're prepared to spend as much on wheels as my daily driver 85 GT cost me. Actually, some of the best tire bargains and size selection is in the 16 inch range.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula||01-02-2000||11:08 AM||||If you have spacers making the tire stick out too far doesn't that stress the cv joint?<P>Just a thought.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||TwisT||01-02-2000||12:52 PM||||...ok...  18" inch wheels on a Fiero...  Pictures...  anyone have any?  =)  That was my original question...<P>...A couple of comments.  Wheel fitment today,  whether it be on a Fiero or a Ferrari,  is not a question but the norm.  Pick up an issue of Super Street,  Sport Compact Car,  etc,  and you will rarely find a car without AT LEAST 17" rims.  The trend now is "taller,  wider" and is becoming as common place,  much like 15" rims of the '80's.  Just think;  those 17" rims used to be looked at as "extremely exotic and ultra expensive" and only available to those with tons of cash to burn.  Now you can pick up a nice set for under $700!  18"'s are following suit;  finding an "inexpensive" set is getting easier and easier to do.  <P>Realisticly?  If you REALLY wanted to be different,  there is no reason you couldn't install 20" wheels in the rear and 19" wheels in the front (gulp!).  It is all merely correct sizing and backspacing of the wheel and tire.  Wheel spacers are not necessary if the combination used remains compliant to the original specs.  Following "plus-sizing" fitment means your rim/ tire remains under the fender where they belong.<P>Again...  PICTURES?  lol...  Someone out there has 'em...  <P>  ||||reg||4
Z||000010||Spyder||01-02-2000||02:00 PM||||If I were you, TwisT, since you have a 2" drop in the front, I would go with 17s on the front and go with 18s on the rear. Or even 19s on the rear. On the cars that I've seen with the 17s with droped front,there's not much room but you can drive it. 18s on the front might work if you keep the width off the wheel small and use a narrow and low profile tire.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||TwisT||01-02-2000||03:50 PM||||Right on,  Spyder...  here is my current wheel/tire combo selection:<P>Konig Tantrums:<P>19x8.5" rear:  45mm offset,  245/35-19 Dunlop<BR>18x7" front:  40mm offset,  215/35-18 Nitto<P>Keep in mind,  this combo is a direct stock-replacement,  and does not take into consideration the added clearance gained from the rear coil-overs.  Using more offset,  I'd love to see a 265/35-19 fit in the rear...  <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000012||Eric||01-02-2000||04:04 PM||||TwisT, you're better off going to the Tire Rack to find your pictures.  They have a feature where you can see any wheel/tire combination they stock on any car (I think Fieros are included).  I personally haven't seen any Fieros with larger than 17 inch wheels.  You may be able to get away with 18s on the 84-87 cars and still improve the handling, but the 88s are much harder to work with when it comes to retaining the optimal suspension geometry.  Why would you want to spend all that money on drop spindles and coil overs and not end up with a better handling car for it?||||reg||1
Z||000013||TwisT||01-02-2000||04:20 PM||||...The Tire Rack wheel machine doesn't have a Fiero to use (at least in GT fastback form).  Their wheel selection is also based on only stock-replacement numbers which are put into their computer...  not much help.<P>The '88 GT's suspension is different to modifiy than it's '84 to '87 stablemates...  but responds equally well to mods.  You will notice an enhanced road feel on the '88,  where as the pre-'88's only feel "stiffer,"  perhaps less refined ride.  I've owned and driven both;  and will take the '88 over pre-'88's any day.  Plus-sizing adds to the performance feel and handling,  not take away from it.  If your suspension sucks though,  it will seem worse!  Money well spent,  coming from the mouth of experience...  =)<BR>  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Eric||01-03-2000||12:56 AM||||I prefer Discount Tire over Tire Crack anyway, TwisT.  I'm glad you have some knowledge on the 88 and are willing to put your money where your mouth is.  I have an 88GT also, and I would like to upgrade the wheels someday.  I was thinking 17s, but if you can make 18's work, it could be worth a shot.  Anyway, I'd like to hear all your mods suspension and otherwise. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000015||TwisT||01-03-2000||01:50 AM||||Eric,  your '88 is already set-up (suspension wise) with superior componants.  One important note:  I'm going with coil-overs (rear) and spindles in front to keep the geometry correct.  Lowering springs suck;  they make your ride harsh and negetively effect your suspension's ability to do it's job.<P>Too bad coil-overs and spindles aren't as dirt cheap as springs!  =) ||||reg||6
Z||000016||william_thorniii||01-03-2000||12:03 PM||||Twist,<P>Are you a member of the fiero-list?  If so you should talk to MWS.  If not, contact me and I will ask him if he would mind discussing this subject with you.  It has been a while since I have seen his spec sheet on his car, but I think that he is currently running on 17" wheels with 1.5" Ryane drop spindles coupled to their slalom suspension... that is IF I remember correctly.  Perhaps he could help you. Although I have studied chassis design, I am only in the early stages of designing the chassis on my 85 GT and am therefore not a good reference. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000017||TwisT||01-03-2000||02:40 PM||||...right on!  MWS is who helped me out the most.  He know's his stuff...||||reg||14
Z||000018||Eric||01-03-2000||08:46 PM||||Damn!  We need to get him over here.  I wonder if Cyber-kidnapping is a crime.||||reg||1
Z||000019||TwisT||01-03-2000||09:11 PM||||MWS is a busy man...  maybe we could bribe him.  lol<P>...Anyone have pictures out there in Fiero-land???  ||||reg||12
Z||000020||Monkeyman||01-04-2000||05:21 AM||||I've got pics of Fieros with 17s, but 18 are kinda rare.  I've got one pic somewhere of a Fiero with 20s on the rear and 19s on the front but the car's been modified so much that it's hard to tell that it's still a Fiero.  If I can find it, I'll email it to you (I've got well over 1000 pics-maybe as many as 1250 by now-and keep them in separate folders.  some are organized and some aren't).||||reg||1
Z||000021||loafer87gt||01-04-2000||11:21 AM||||TwistT: Let me know when you get your wheels put on. I was looking at 17x7 Tantrums for my 87 GT. They are a sweet wheel, they kinda remind me of the older TSW Evolutions. What kind of coil overs are you going with? I checked with Ground Control and it turns out they have a kit for rear of the Fiero. They are priced real competitive as well. I'll dig up the the link to their site and post it to you if your interested.  Good luck with your rims, should look sweet when done.||||reg||1
Z||000022||TwisT||01-04-2000||08:33 PM||||loafer,  the suspension componants I'm going to install on my '88 are from Ryane Motorsports.  Questions of quality and service keep me away from other's products.  Darrel stands behind his products,  and have been proven time and again to be of the best that's out there.  ||||reg||7
